### ============================================================
### LAB 4 — Transactional Outbox & Event-Driven Architecture
### ============================================================
### Base URLs:
###   Customer Service  → http://localhost:8001
###   Policy Service    → http://localhost:8002
###   Notification Svc  → http://localhost:8003
###   RabbitMQ UI       → http://localhost:15672  (guest/guest)


# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STEP 1 — Create customers (triggers CustomerCreated events)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### 1.1 Create Customer Alice
POST http://localhost:8001/api/v1/customers/
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Alice Smith2",
  "email": "alice@example.com2"
}

###

### 1.2 Create Customer Bob
POST http://localhost:8001/api/v1/customers/
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Bob Jones",
  "email": "bob@example.com"
}

###

### 1.3 List all customers
GET http://localhost:8001/api/v1/customers/

###


# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STEP 2 — Create policies (triggers PolicyCreated events)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### 2.1 Create policy for Alice (customer_id=1) with Correlation ID
POST http://localhost:8002/api/v1/policies/
Content-Type: application/json
X-Correlation-ID: trace-alice-health-001

{
  "customer_id": 1,
  "policy_type": "Health Insurance"
}

###

### 2.2 Create policy for Bob (customer_id=2)
POST http://localhost:8002/api/v1/policies/
Content-Type: application/json
X-Correlation-ID: trace-bob-auto-001

{
  "customer_id": 2,
  "policy_type": "Auto Insurance"
}

###

### 2.3 Try to create policy for non-existent customer → 404
POST http://localhost:8002/api/v1/policies/
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customer_id": 9999,
  "policy_type": "Life Insurance"
}

###


# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STEP 3 — Verify events were delivered to Notification Service
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### 3.1 List processed notifications
GET http://localhost:8003/api/v1/notifications

###


# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STEP 4 — RELIABILITY TEST (Transactional Outbox demo)
#
# Instructions:
#   1. Stop RabbitMQ:  docker compose stop rabbitmq
#   2. Run request 4.1 below — policy IS saved, event stays PENDING in outbox
#   3. Check DB directly (optional):
#        docker exec -it policy_db psql -U user -d policy_db \
#          -c "SELECT id, event_type, status FROM outbox_messages;"
#   4. Restart RabbitMQ:  docker compose start rabbitmq
#   5. Wait ~5 seconds for the relay to sweep
#   6. Run request 3.1 again — notification WILL appear automatically
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### 4.1 Create policy WHILE broker is stopped
###     (event saved in outbox DB, delivered automatically when broker returns)
POST http://localhost:8002/api/v1/policies/
Content-Type: application/json
X-Correlation-ID: reliability-test-001

{
  "customer_id": 1,
  "policy_type": "Life Insurance"
}

###


# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STEP 5 — Inspect outbox tables directly
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### 5.1 Customer outbox — all messages
# Run in terminal:
# docker exec -it customer_db psql -U user -d customer_db \
#   -c "SELECT id, event_type, status, sent_at FROM outbox_messages ORDER BY id;"

### 5.2 Policy outbox — all messages
# docker exec -it policy_db psql -U user -d policy_db \
#   -c "SELECT id, event_type, status, sent_at, error FROM outbox_messages ORDER BY id;"

###


